.TH IOWATCHD 8 "October 2026" "iowatchd 0.3.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
iowatchd \- monitor disk IO usage and log which processes consume it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iowatchd
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iowatchd
periodically measures the IO utilization of a disk (the \fBio_ticks\fR field of
\fI/proc/diskstats\fR, equivalent to \fB%util\fR in \fBiostat\fR(1)). When the
utilization goes above the configured threshold, it measures the IO of every
process (\fI/proc/<pid>/io\fR) and logs the ones doing the most reads and
writes, with name, PID, user, rates and command line.
.PP
Entries go to standard output (captured by journald) and to a log file with
simple rotation. Seeing all processes requires running as root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR
Configuration file. Default: \fI/etc/iowatchd.conf\fR, if it exists, or the
file named by \fB$IOWATCHD_CONFIG\fR. A file chosen explicitly must exist.
Files matching \fIFILE\fR\fB.d/*.conf\fR are read afterwards, in alphabetical
order, and override it.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-device\fR \fIDISK\fR
Disk to monitor (\fBsda\fR, \fBnvme0n1\fR...). Default \fBauto\fR: the physical
disk that holds the root partition.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-threshold\fR \fIPCT\fR
IO usage, in %, that triggers logging (1 to 100). Default 80.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interval\fR \fISEC\fR
Seconds between samples (minimum 1). Default 2.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-top\fR \fIN\fR
Number of processes listed in each report. Default 5.
.TP
\fB\-j\fR, \fB\-\-json\fR
Write one JSON object per line (keys \fBtime\fR, \fBevent\fR, \fBmessage\fR and,
for alerts, a \fBprocesses\fR array) instead of plain text.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-once\fR
Take a single measurement, print the result to standard output and exit.
.TP
\fB\-C\fR, \fB\-\-check\-config\fR
Validate the configuration, print the effective settings and exit. Exits with
status 1 if there are errors or warnings.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-foreground\fR
Run in the foreground. This is already the default; the option is accepted for
compatibility with service managers.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Do not print to standard output (the log file is still written).
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Also print every sample to standard output.
.TP
\fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-list\-devices\fR
List the disks that can be monitored (name, size, SSD/HDD, model) and mark the
one chosen by \fBauto\fR. Exit.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help and exit.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Show the version, license and project URL, and exit.
.PP
Short options can be grouped (\fB\-jq\fR) and take attached values
(\fB\-t80\fR). \fB\-\-\fR ends the options; mistyped long options get a
suggestion. Command-line options take precedence over the configuration file.
.SH CONFIGURATION
See \fBiowatchd.conf\fR(5).
.SH NOTIFICATIONS
Under systemd (\fBType=notify\fR) the daemon sends \fBREADY=1\fR once it is
sampling, a status line, and \fBSTOPPING=1\fR on shutdown, using
\fB$NOTIFY_SOCKET\fR. Without that variable nothing is sent.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the configuration without restarting. If the new configuration is
invalid, the current one is kept.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Log the current status (device, last IO usage, state) without interrupting
monitoring.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Stop cleanly.
.PP
\fBSIGPIPE\fR has its default action, so \fBiowatchd \-\-once | head\fR ends
quietly.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B IOWATCHD_CONFIG
Configuration file to use when \fB\-c\fR is not given.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime error (unreadable configuration, missing disk).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (invalid option).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/iowatchd.conf
Configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/iowatchd.conf.d/*.conf
Drop-in configuration overrides.
.TP
.I /var/log/iowatchd.log
Alert log.
.SH NOTES
Without root privileges (or \fBCAP_SYS_PTRACE\fR) only the processes of the
current user can be inspected; a warning is printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
iowatchd \-\-once
iowatchd \-d nvme0n1 \-t 60 \-\-once
iowatchd \-\-list\-devices
journalctl \-u iowatchd \-f
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR iowatchd.conf (5),
.BR iowatchd-log (1),
.BR iostat (1),
.BR iotop (8)
